+# callee-saved and preserved across the trampoline's `blr` call (the
+# allocator's x30 is saved on its stack at [sp,#0x70] and restored by the
+# epilogue at 0x95448c, so the trampoline's `blr` clobbering x30 is harmless
+# -- the trampoline JUMPS to 0x95448c, it does not `ret`). The trampoline
+# writes only x0/x1/x2/x8/x9 (caller-saved, dead after the V call in the
+# original code: x9 held an intermediate of the allocator's size
+# computation and the epilogue at 0x95448c never reads it) and x23 (the
+# return value, set deliberately). sp is never touched, so the grow request
+# at [sp,#8] is intact for the fail path. The `cbz` checks are the 32-bit
+# form (as in 0239/0241): a NULL pointer is 0, a valid heap pointer has
+# non-zero low 32 bits; the count is an i32, so `cbz w1` tests count == 0.
+# The `cmp`/`b.eq` guard: `cbz` does not set flags, so the `cmp` sets them
+# fresh and `b.eq` reads them; no flag-clobbering instruction sits between.
+# UNPINNED: whether array[0] is the ExpHeap (the desired case) cannot be
+# decided from static disassembly alone -- the list contents are runtime
+# values, and the "first registered = root ExpHeap" step is the sead
+# creation order (the root heap is created first, before any FrameHeap),
+# corroborated by the game allocating its main heap FROM array[0] at
+# 0x9436d0, but not proven by a single instruction. The guard covers the
+# FrameHeap case (vtable == 0xdbdf00 -> fall back to V, abort as before),
+# and an array[0] that cannot hold the SARC (allocate returns 0) also
+# falls back to V, so the fix is safe either way; but if array[0] is not
+# a heap that can hold 946,368 bytes, the costumes still abort and the fix
+# does not help. The mock verifies the logic on host values only. Device
+# acceptance: Explorer / Football / Mechanic enter a kingdom without the
+# abort backtrace and without a codesigning kill, and the good costumes
+# (Happi / Hakama / Armor / Painter) are unaffected (they never reach the
+# overflow path, so this cut is a no-op for them).
